# six values
site/urls.go:65:6: view A takes 6 values and returns 2
  fix: write func(ctx context.Context, [u User,] [db maago.DB | tx maago.Tx,] in In) (Page or maago.Result[Page], error), or func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request)

# In of another package
site/urls.go:65:32: the In of view A is not struct{} or a struct type named in package site
  fix: declare type AIn struct{…} in package site with fields tagged maago:"path=name", maago:"query=name" or maago:"form", or take struct{}; if it is the user, In comes after it: func A(ctx context.Context, u http.Request, in struct{}) …

# no In after the user
site/more.go:12:31: the In of view A is not struct{} or a struct type named in package site
  fix: declare type AIn struct{…} in package site with fields tagged maago:"path=name", maago:"query=name" or maago:"form", or take struct{}; if it is the user, In comes after it: func A(ctx context.Context, u users.User, in struct{}) …

# no In after the database
site/more.go:12:47: view A takes no In: In comes last, even when empty
  fix: write func A(ctx context.Context, u *users.User, db maago.DB, in struct{}) …

# database before the user
site/more.go:12:32: view A takes its values out of order
  fix: write func A(ctx context.Context, u users.User, db maago.DB, in struct{}) …

# two databases
site/more.go:12:37: view A takes its values out of order
  fix: write func A(ctx context.Context, db maago.DB, in struct{}) …

# login twice
site/urls.go:11:26: there is no maago.LoginRequired: the view's user says it
  fix: remove it: taking u users.User already requires login

# login with a user that may be missing
site/urls.go:11:26: there is no maago.LoginRequired: the view's user says it
  fix: remove it, and take u users.User, not *users.User: the route then requires login

# login on one route of a view
site/urls.go:11:57: there is no maago.LoginRequired: the view's user says it
  fix: remove it, and split the view: a view for route a_post takes u users.User, which requires login; the other keeps u *users.User

# untagged field
site/urls.go:65:18: field X says not where its value comes from
  fix: tag it with one source: maago:"path=name", maago:"query=name" or maago:"form"

# unknown key
site/urls.go:65:34: unknown key "qery" in the tag of X
  fix: did you mean "query"? allowed: path, query, form

# rest of the path
site/urls.go:11:12: route a reads the rest of the path with {p...}, which v0 does not support
  fix: split the route, or read the rest of the path with a func(w, r) view

# two fields of one wildcard
site/urls.go:67:2: two fields of AIn read path value "x"
  fix: keep one

# wildcard without a field
site/urls.go:11:12: view A reads no {x} of route a
  fix: add a field tagged maago:"path=x" to the In of A

# field without a wildcard
site/urls.go:11:12: route a has no {x}, which AIn.X reads
  fix: add {x} to the path, or remove the field

# path types
site/urls.go:68:4: path value X cannot be read into float64
  fix: use one of string, int64, int32
site/urls.go:69:4: path value Y cannot be read into ID
  fix: use one of string, int64, int32
site/urls.go:70:4: path value Z cannot be read into *int64
  fix: use one of string, int64, int32

# query types
site/urls.go:66:4: query value X cannot be read into []string
  fix: use one of string, int64, int32, bool, float64, or a pointer to one when the value may be missing
site/urls.go:67:4: query value Y cannot be read into **int64
  fix: use one of string, int64, int32, bool, float64, or a pointer to one when the value may be missing

# two views wrong, a route name taken
site/urls.go:11:43: route name "a" is taken by site/urls.go:11
  fix: route names are one namespace across the project; rename one
site/urls.go:65:18: field X says not where its value comes from
  fix: tag it with one source: maago:"path=name", maago:"query=name" or maago:"form"

# names clash
site/more.go:15:5: strconv is declared at the top level, but maago's generated files in this package may import a package strconv
  fix: rename it
site/urls.go:10:5: the routes of app site would declare App, which site/more.go:14 declares already
  fix: rename what else declares App; maago generates each app's App
site/urls.go:10:5: the templates of app site would declare maagoPages, which site/more.go:17 declares already
  fix: rename what else declares maagoPages
site/urls.go:12:27: route post_detail would declare PostDetailURL, which site/more.go:12 declares already
  fix: rename the route, or what else declares that name

# In with fields, not named
site/urls.go:65:32: the In of view A is not struct{} or a struct type named in package site
  fix: declare type AIn struct{…} in package site with fields tagged maago:"path=name", maago:"query=name" or maago:"form", or take struct{}; if it is the user, In comes after it: func A(ctx context.Context, u struct{X int64}, in struct{}) …

# two sources
site/urls.go:65:42: field X names more than one source
  fix: keep one: maago:"path=name", maago:"query=name" or maago:"form"

# embedded field
site/urls.go:65:18: In of view A embeds a field
  fix: name each field, tagged maago:"path=…" or maago:"query=…"

# generic view
site/urls.go:65:6: view A has type parameters
  fix: write func(ctx context.Context, [u User,] [db maago.DB | tx maago.Tx,] in In) (Page or maago.Result[Page], error), or func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request)

# first not a context
site/urls.go:65:6: view A takes int first, not a context.Context
  fix: write func(ctx context.Context, [u User,] [db maago.DB | tx maago.Tx,] in In) (Page or maago.Result[Page], error), or func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request)
  see: maago doc view

# sql.DB for the database
site/more.go:12:31: view A takes the database as *sql.DB, not maago.DB
  fix: write func A(ctx context.Context, u users.User, db maago.DB, in struct{}) …

# a transaction on a GET
site/more.go:12:32: view A takes maago.Tx, but route a is a GET, which does not write
  fix: take db maago.DB; a view that writes serves a POST

# a transaction and the database
site/more.go:12:31: view A takes both maago.Tx and maago.DB
  fix: write func A(ctx context.Context, u users.User, tx maago.Tx, in struct{}) …: the transaction reads and writes

# sql.Tx
site/more.go:12:32: view A takes the transaction as *sql.Tx, not maago.Tx
  fix: write func A(ctx context.Context, tx maago.Tx, in struct{}) …

# NoTx on a GET
site/urls.go:11:26: route a has maago.NoTx, but a GET writes nothing
  fix: remove maago.NoTx

# NoTx with a transaction
site/urls.go:11:27: route a has maago.NoTx, but view A takes maago.Tx
  fix: remove maago.NoTx, or take db maago.DB

# NoTx without the database
site/urls.go:11:27: route a has maago.NoTx, but view A takes no maago.DB
  fix: take db maago.DB, or remove maago.NoTx

# NoTx on a func(w, r)
site/urls.go:11:27: route a has maago.NoTx, but view A is a func(w, r), which has no transaction
  fix: remove maago.NoTx

# NoTx on one route of a view
site/urls.go:11:27: route a has maago.NoTx, but route a_put of the same view A does not
  fix: give both maago.NoTx, or split the view

# a pointer to maago.DB
site/more.go:12:32: view A takes the database as *maago.DB, not maago.DB
  fix: write func A(ctx context.Context, db maago.DB, in struct{}) …

# login on one route of a view that requires it
site/urls.go:11:57: there is no maago.LoginRequired: the view's user says it
  fix: remove it: taking u users.User already requires login

# login on a view without the user
site/urls.go:11:26: there is no maago.LoginRequired: the view's user says it
  fix: remove it, and take u U in the view (the route then requires login; u *U lets anybody in)

# login on a func(w, r)
site/urls.go:11:26: there is no maago.LoginRequired: the view's user says it
  fix: remove it; a func(w, r) view reads maago.UserFrom itself and answers 401 or 303

# the user from ctx
site/more.go:13:10: view A calls maago.UserFrom: a typed view takes the user as a parameter
  fix: take u users.User between ctx and the database (u *users.User when it may be missing)

# the database from ctx in a closure
site/more.go:13:20: view A calls maago.DBFrom: a typed view takes the database as a parameter
  fix: take db maago.DB (tx maago.Tx to write) between ctx and in

# a write on the database
site/urls.go:11:2: route a writes, but view A takes maago.DB, which runs outside a transaction
  fix: take tx maago.Tx

# two writes on the database
site/urls.go:11:2: route a writes, but view A takes maago.DB, which runs outside a transaction
  fix: take tx maago.Tx

# a pointer that is no user
site/more.go:12:6: view A takes *context.Context, which is not a user type
  fix: between ctx and In a view takes the user, a struct of the project such as auth.User (*auth.User if it may be missing), then db maago.DB
  see: maago doc view

# a string for the user
site/more.go:12:6: view A takes string, which is not a user type
  fix: between ctx and In a view takes the user, a struct of the project such as auth.User (*auth.User if it may be missing), then db maago.DB
  see: maago doc view

# two user types
site/more.go:14:6: view B takes the user as users.Other, but view A takes users.User
  fix: Config.Auth returns one user type; take it in every view
  see: maago doc view

# second not an error
site/urls.go:65:6: view A returns int second, not an error
  fix: write func(ctx context.Context, [u User,] [db maago.DB | tx maago.Tx,] in In) (Page or maago.Result[Page], error), or func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request)
  see: maago doc view

# page unnamed
site/urls.go:65:6: view A returns struct{}, which names no template
  fix: return a struct type declared in package site: its name names the template (PostDetailPage → templates/post_detail_page.html)
  see: maago doc view

# page of another package
site/urls.go:65:6: view A returns net/http.Request, which names no template
  fix: return a struct type declared in package site: its name names the template (PostDetailPage → templates/post_detail_page.html)
  see: maago doc view

# generic page
site/urls.go:67:6: view A returns G[int], which names no template
  fix: return a struct type declared in package site: its name names the template (PostDetailPage → templates/post_detail_page.html)
  see: maago doc view

# page not a struct
site/urls.go:67:6: view A returns P, which is not a struct
  fix: make the page a struct type
  see: maago doc view

# view in a file go build leaves out
site/urls.go:13:6: view A is in a file go build leaves out
  fix: remove the file's build constraint, or move the view to a file without one
  see: maago doc view

# a broken file no page uses
site/templates/base.html:1: bad character U+007D '}'
  fix: fix the template; it is Go's html/template
  see: maago doc template

# a template name in other letters
site/templates/Home_Page.html: template Home_Page.html is no page's and not base.html
  fix: name it after a page type (PostPage → post_page.html), or move what it holds into a {{define}} of base.html
  see: maago doc template
site/urls.go:22:6: page HomePage has no template site/templates/home_page.html
  fix: create it; a page type's template is its name in snake_case
  see: maago doc template

# func(w, r) of other types
site/urls.go:65:6: view A takes int and string, not an http.ResponseWriter and an *http.Request
  fix: write func(ctx context.Context, [u User,] [db maago.DB | tx maago.Tx,] in In) (Page or maago.Result[Page], error), or func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request)
  see: maago doc view

# no template
site/urls.go:22:6: page HomePage has no template site/templates/home_page.html
  fix: create it; a page type's template is its name in snake_case
  see: maago doc template

# template syntax
site/templates/home_page.html:2: bad character U+007D '}'
  fix: fix the template; it is Go's html/template
  see: maago doc template

# a type error first
site/more.go:3:13: cannot use "x" (untyped string constant) as int value in variable declaration
  fix: go build reports the same error; fix it there

